unit AMQP.ServerWalTests;

{ Tests for the WAL file layer (AMQP.Server.Wal) -- WS1 of Phase 4
  (durability, see CLAUDE.md, decisions D19-D28).

  They are pure unit tests: none of them starts any broker.

  TWO THINGS HERE ARE NOT ORDINARY TESTS, and deserve an explanation:

  1. LAYER 1 of D28 is TWO exhaustive sweeps, and they are the phase's anchor
     tests. Neither picks "representative" points: the crash picks its own.

       TruncamentoExaustivo  cuts the log at EVERY possible offset and requires
                             recovery to return exactly the records that
                             fit whole there. Models the SHORT write.
       CorrupcaoExaustiva    flips one bit in EVERY byte of the log and requires
                             recovery to stop at the record before the damaged one.
                             Models the sector written WRONG.

     Both are needed, and that was MEASURED, not assumed: with the CRC check
     removed, the truncation sweep stays green. That is because a cut-off tail
     is caught earlier, by the length check -- the CRC only matters
     when the bytes ARE there and are wrong, which is the case only the
     corruption sweep produces. Until that was discovered, the comment here
     claimed the first one covered the CRC; it did not.

  2. TAMQPWalFileFalso is LAYER 2 of D28. Since the segment talks to IAMQPWalFile
     and not to the file system, we can count the fsyncs and fail at the
     chosen moment -- and so prove (a) that a new segment's header
     goes to disk right away and (b) that a write failure does not leave the segment
     with state ahead of the file. Neither would be
     observable from outside.

  DUnitX master in FPCUnit's dialect (TAssert.*); the FPCUnit mirror in
  fpc\ is generated by tools/gen_fpc_mirror.py. }


interface

uses
  DUnitX.TestFramework,
  AMQP.DUnitXCompat,
  AMQP.TestSupport,
  SysUtils, Classes,
  PascalCommon.Threading,
  AMQP.Threading,
  AMQP.Server.Wal,
